{"status":"confirmed","reasoning":"Along y=x the limit is 1/2, so I is false. Along y=x^2 the quotient is 1/(1+x^2), approaching 1, so II is true. Different path limits make III false. Thus II only, option d, matches the earlier answer and Math Academy's worked solution. The captured submission was I only, option a; its Incorrect grade is consistent.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"selection","correct_value":"II only","correct_option":"d","value_type":"text","wrong_value":"I only","correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"II only","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"- Statement I is false. Taking $x\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\to (0,0)\,\,\,y=x}\,\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\to 0}\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\  & ={lim}_{x\to 0}\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\  & =\frac{1}{2}.\end{aligned}
$$

- Statement II is true. Taking $x\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\to (0,0)\,\,\,y={x}^{2}}\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\to 0}\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\  & ={lim}_{x\to 0}\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\  & ={lim}_{x\to 0}\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\  & =\frac{1}{1+0} \\  & =1\end{aligned}
$$

- Statement III is false.","disagrees":false}]}
